    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay thanks, that makes more sense. so if the agent is just a neutral third party, what happens if something goes wrong? like if the seller's side fails to deliver or if there's a dispute over the condition of the house? who actually decides when that money gets released?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If something goes wrong, such as the seller failing to deliver or a dispute over the condition of the house, the escrow agent's role becomes even more critical. The escrow agent will typically follow the instructions laid out in the contract. If the contract specifies that the seller must complete certain repairs or inspections before the funds are released, the escrow agent will hold the money until those conditions are met.\n\nIn the case of a dispute, the escrow agent may need to involve legal counsel or a mediator to help resolve the issue. The escrow agent acts as a neutral party, but they may also have to follow the dispute resolution process outlined in the contract or by law. If the dispute cannot be resolved, it may need to be taken to court, and a judge will make the final decision on when the funds should be released.\n\nIt's important to note that the escrow agent's primary role is to ensure that the conditions of the contract are met before releasing the funds. They are not there to make decisions about the validity of the contract or the condition of the house. Those decisions are typically made by the buyer and seller, or a court if necessary. The escrow agent simply acts as a facilitator to ensure that the agreed-upon terms are followed.",
      "source": "target",
